          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a cough for two weeks after a cold.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The red-flag rule table did not match your description, so this is not an emergency pattern. That is not a clearance. If your symptoms become severe, get worse, or new symptoms appear that you can't explain, seek medical advice promptly.\n\n**Features to get reviewed sooner:** My search for a full red-flag list failed, so I can only point to what the retrieved sources say.\n- **A worsening pattern after improvement.** The influenza summary notes that secondary bacterial pneumonia classically shows up as deterioration after several days of getting better (*Influenza*, General clinical reference).\n- **A cough that is constant, or does not respond to treatment.** The asthma summary says that a cough that never responds to a bronchodilator, or that is constant, calls for considering another explanation (*Asthma*, General clinical reference).\n- **Smoking or other lung exposures.** Persistent cough with sputum in someone with relevant exposures is a reason to consider chronic lung disease (*COPD*, General clinical reference).\n\n**How it's grouped:** Your symptom falls in one body system, the respiratory system. Based on the sources, the main possibilities a clinician might consider are:\n- **Post-viral cough.** The common cold guidance says cough often lasts two to three weeks and is normal on its own (*Upper respiratory infection*, General clinical reference; *Antibiotic stewardship guidance*).\n- **Asthma**, which can be triggered by viral infections and often causes night-time or early-morning cough.\n- **COPD**, which is mainly relevant if you have a smoking or exposure history.\n\nI can't tell you which of these, if any, applies to you. A clinician can assess that, usually by listening to your chest and asking about your history. Book an appointment if the cough is not clearly improving after about three weeks, or sooner if any of the warning signs above apply.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
