          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would antibiotics help?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: for most common colds and coughs, antibiotics would not help.**\n\n**Viral infections.** The antibiotic stewardship guidance says most acute upper respiratory infections are viral. These include the common cold, most sore throats, and acute bronchitis. Antibiotics do not shorten them and do not prevent complications at a rate that justifies routine use (*Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship*, guideline). The general reference on the common cold says the same: antibiotics have no effect on viral infection, and standard care is rest, fluids and symptom relief (*Upper respiratory infection (common cold)*, General clinical reference). Influenza is also viral, and the influenza guidance says antibiotics have no activity against it. They are reserved for bacterial complications (*Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment*, guideline).\n\n**Bacterial infections.** Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are used when a clinician thinks a bacterial infection is likely or has been confirmed. The sources I searched do not describe the full list of situations where this applies, so I can't give you a complete picture here. Some guidance also says that a bacterial complication can appear after a viral illness has seemed to improve.\n\n**Harms.** Antibiotics can cause side effects, allergic reactions, and bowel infection. They also encourage resistant bacteria, which makes future infections harder to treat (*Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship*, guideline).\n\nTo make this more specific, please tell me which condition or symptoms you mean. For example: a cough, a sore throat, a urinary symptom, or something else. The guidance differs by condition, and I can then check what the sources say about that one.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
